Cut MTP round trips: read ahead, cache storage info, trust cached attributes.

Every MTP transaction costs ~1.5 ms of fixed overhead on top of its payload (on
a Xiaomi 17 Pro over USB 2.0 a 16 KiB read takes 1.8 ms, a 1 MiB read 27-29 ms).
Copying a photo issued one GetPartialObject per FUSE request, one more
transaction for open(), and a GetStorageInfo for every statfs() - which Finder,
the Storage settings pane and copyfile issue constantly.

- Read through a small LRU of read-ahead buffers. The first read of a file and
  every seek fetch exactly what was asked for; a reader that continues where
  the previous fetch ended gets up to 8 MiB at once (1 MiB requests from cp and
  Finder jump straight there, smaller ones double each time). Writes,
  truncation and unlink drop the buffer for that object.
- Answer statfs from a 5 second cache of GetStorageInfo.
- Decide EISDIR in open() from cached attributes instead of asking the device.
- Append without TruncateObject: Android's SendPartialObject extends the object
  when it writes at the end, so only a write past the end needs the gap filled.

Measured on a Xiaomi 17 Pro over USB 2.0, interleaving runs of 100 different
photos each: cp from the volume ran 30.8 / 30.7 / 30.8 MiB/s without this patch
and 32.7 / 32.5 / 33.5 MiB/s with it, and 60 patterned and whole-file read
hashes matched. Reading the first 64 KiB of a photo through the page cache
stays one 1 MiB transaction, ~40 ms either way; a version that fetched 8 MiB
for any 1 MiB request took ~226 ms for that read.

